    {
      "section_id": "core_biopsy_device_section_1",
      "section_title": "Core biopsy device",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A core biopsy device is a medical instrument designed to obtain cylindrical tissue samples from a lesion for histopathological examination. It is commonly used in breast, prostate, liver, kidney, lymph node and other soft-tissue biopsies, depending on the device design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In breast diagnostics, it is particularly useful for investigating suspicious breast lumps and abnormal findings on mammography, ultrasound or MRI."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike fine-needle aspiration (FNA), which primarily collects individual cells or fluid, a core biopsy device obtains tissue fragments that preserve the tissue architecture. This helps pathologists determine the type of lesion and, when applicable, distinguish in-situ from invasive malignancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a core biopsy device, used to obtain cylindrical tissue samples from a suspicious lesion for histopathological examination. It may be manually operated or automated, and sampling is often performed under image guidance.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Identification and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise components vary between automated spring-loaded devices, reusable systems and vacuum-assisted systems. For a typical automated core biopsy device, the following are the important parts."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "minervahealthinc.com"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Handle or body",
                      "Provides a grip and houses the firing mechanism."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Trigger or firing button",
                      "Activates the sampling mechanism."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cocking mechanism",
                      "Prepares the spring-loaded device for firing, where applicable."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inner stylet",
                      "Advances into the lesion and carries the sampling notch."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sampling notch",
                      "Receives the tissue intended for collection."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Outer cutting cannula",
                      "Cuts the tissue core as it advances over the notch."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Needle shaft",
                      "Provides access to the target lesion."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sharp distal tip",
                      "Facilitates penetration into the target."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Depth indicator or stop",
                      "Helps control insertion depth on models that include it."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Coaxial introducer",
                      "Provides an access tract for repeated sampling when used."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: The stylet and cutting cannula are distinct components. The notch receives tissue, while the cutting cannula severs it. Not all biopsy devices have the same trigger arrangement or depth-control features."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Properties and characteristics",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Typically medical-grade stainless steel or other biocompatible materials."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterility: Commonly supplied sterile and designed for single use, although some systems have reusable components."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gauge: A 14G needle is commonly used for breast core biopsy; other gauges are available."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mechanism: Manual, spring-loaded automated, semi-automated or vacuum-assisted."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sampling capability: Produces tissue cores suitable for histopathological analysis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Guidance: May be used under ultrasound, stereotactic mammographic or MRI guidance, provided the device is compatible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Design: May incorporate a disposable needle assembly and a reusable or disposable firing handle."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Manual core biopsy needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses a manually operated cutting mechanism to obtain a tissue core."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected soft-tissue and organ biopsies, depending on the needle design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Limitation: Sampling depends more heavily on the operator's technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Automated core biopsy device"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses a spring-loaded mechanism to advance the sampling components rapidly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Breast masses, lymph nodes and selected solid-organ lesions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantage: Rapid and relatively consistent sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Semi-automated core biopsy device"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Typically uses a staged mechanism in which one component is positioned before the cutting mechanism is activated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected lesions where controlled positioning is desirable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantage: Allows the operator to position the sampling notch before cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/core_biopsy_device_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Vacuum-assisted biopsy device"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses suction to draw tissue into a sampling aperture before cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Breast microcalcifications, selected small lesions and situations requiring multiple samples."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantage: Multiple specimens can often be obtained through one access tract."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The core biopsy device works by capturing and cutting a small cylinder of tissue from the target lesion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For a typical automated device:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The lesion is identified by palpation or imaging."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The skin is prepared using aseptic technique, and local anaesthesia is administered where appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The needle is advanced to the target using the chosen guidance method."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The stylet advances, positioning the sampling notch within the lesion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cutting cannula moves over the notch, severing the tissue core."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The specimen is retrieved and inspected."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Additional samples are obtained if indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The specimens are labelled and sent for histopathological examination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact firing sequence varies by device; the manufacturer's instructions must be followed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Indications and uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The core biopsy device is used for:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diagnosis of suspicious breast lumps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Investigation of suspicious mammographic or ultrasound findings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sampling of breast microcalcifications using an appropriate image-guided system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Histological evaluation of suspicious lymph nodes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Biopsy of selected liver, kidney and other solid-organ lesions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sampling of selected soft-tissue masses."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diagnosis of suspected malignancy before definitive treatment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtaining tissue for tumour classification and biomarker testing when appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The gauge, needle length, approach and imaging modality depend on the target organ, lesion characteristics and procedural risks."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Surgeries and procedures in which it is used",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The device is principally used in percutaneous image-guided biopsy procedures, rather than as a standard instrument for open surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examples include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ultrasound-guided breast core needle biopsy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stereotactic breast biopsy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "MRI-guided breast biopsy with a compatible device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ultrasound-guided lymph node biopsy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Image-guided liver or renal biopsy with a suitable device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected musculoskeletal or soft-tissue biopsies."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The result may guide subsequent procedures such as lumpectomy, mastectomy or other definitive surgery. The biopsy itself does not normally remove the entire lesion."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides tissue architecture for histopathological assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually requires only a small skin puncture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often performed under local anaesthesia."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be performed as an outpatient procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows accurate targeting when combined with appropriate imaging."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May provide material for immunohistochemistry and other tumour biomarker tests."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Automated systems allow rapid and consistent tissue sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can reduce the need for an initial open surgical biopsy."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bleeding and haematoma may occur."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The device may miss the lesion or obtain inadequate tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some lesions are difficult to access safely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The firing mechanism may cause discomfort or a startling clicking sound."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Larger needles may produce more tissue trauma than fine needles."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some procedures require specialised imaging equipment and trained personnel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A benign or inconclusive result may require repeat biopsy or surgical excision."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Haematoma and bleeding"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important complications, particularly in vascular tissues or patients with bleeding risks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Pain and bruising"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually temporary and managed with appropriate analgesia and local measures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Infection"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uncommon when proper aseptic technique is followed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Sampling error"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect targeting or insufficient tissue may result in a false-negative or non-diagnostic result."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Organ-specific injury"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Depending on the biopsy site, injury to nearby structures may occur. For example, pneumothorax is a recognised risk during certain chest-wall or lung-adjacent procedures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few absolute contraindications, but precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Significant uncorrected coagulopathy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anticoagulant or antiplatelet use requiring individual assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Infection at the puncture site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inability to maintain a safe position during the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lesions near major vessels or other vulnerable structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inadequate access or inability to visualise the target safely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For organ-specific biopsies, additional contraindications and precautions may apply. Anticoagulants should not be stopped without an appropriate clinical plan."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Specimen handling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Proper handling is essential because the purpose of the procedure is to establish an accurate diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the patient's identity and biopsy site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Place tissue cores in the appropriate specimen container."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the fixative specified by the receiving laboratory, commonly neutral buffered formalin for routine histopathology."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Label the specimen accurately, including the anatomical site and laterality where relevant."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provide the clinical history and relevant imaging findings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Arrange specialised specimen handling when microbiology, molecular testing or other investigations are requested."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For breast lesions, the pathology result should be correlated with imaging and clinical findings."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Comparison: core biopsy versus FNA",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Core biopsy",
                    "FNA"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Specimen",
                      "Tissue core",
                      "Cells or fluid"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Examination",
                      "Histopathology",
                      "Cytology"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue architecture",
                      "Preserved",
                      "Not usually preserved"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Needle",
                      "Commonly larger bore",
                      "Fine bore"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Biomarker testing",
                      "Often suitable",
                      "Depends on sample and laboratory methods"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main limitation",
                      "Bleeding and sampling error",
                      "Insufficient information from cytology alone in some cases"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield point: Core biopsy is generally preferred when histological architecture and invasive status are important for diagnosis and treatment planning."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a core biopsy device used to obtain cylindrical tissue samples for histopathological examination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the function of the sampling notch?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It receives the tissue sample before the cutting cannula severs it."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the function of the outer cannula?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It cuts the tissue within the sampling notch, allowing retrieval of a tissue core."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is a common gauge for breast core biopsy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "14G is commonly used, although other sizes are available."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the major difference between an automated and a vacuum-assisted device?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An automated core biopsy gun typically uses a spring-driven cutting mechanism. A vacuum-assisted system uses suction to draw tissue into an aperture and facilitates repeated sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What is the most important common complication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bleeding or haematoma formation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What should you do if histology is benign but the imaging remains suspicious?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Investigate radiology–pathology discordance. Repeat image-guided biopsy or surgical excision may be necessary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why is a core biopsy important in suspected breast cancer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides tissue architecture and may permit histological classification, assessment of invasion and biomarker testing to guide treatment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "core_biopsy_device_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "core_biopsy_device_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Before completing the station, ensure you can:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identify the device and state its primary function."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Label the stylet, sampling notch, cutting cannula, handle and trigger where present."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the principle of tissue sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Classify the major types of core biopsy devices."
                },
                {
